.PP
UI/Effects code splitting pattern
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRread the original article\fP to understand concepts behind\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRread how Google\fP split view and logic\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRwatch how Facebook\fP defers "interactivity" effects\&.
.PP
.SS "Terminology:"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsidecar\fP - non UI component, which may carry effects for a paired UI component\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRUI\fP - UI component, which interactivity is moved to a \fRsidecar\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
\fRUI\fP is a \fIview\fP, \fRsidecar\fP is the \fIlogic\fP for it\&. Like Batman(UI) and his sidekick Robin(effects)\&.
.SS "Concept"
.IP "\(bu" 2
a \fRpackage\fP exposes \fB3 entry points\fP using a \fRnested \fRpackage\&.json\fP format\fP:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
default aka \fRcombination\fP, and lets hope tree shaking will save you
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRUI\fP, with only UI part
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRsidecar\fP, with all the logic
.IP "  \(bu" 4
> \fRUI\fP + \fRsidecar\fP === \fRcombination\fP\&. The size of \fRUI+sidecar\fP might a bit bigger than size of their \fRcombination\fP\&. Use \fRsize-limit\fP to control their size independently\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
package uses a \fRmedium\fP to talk with own sidecar, breaking explicit dependency\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
if package depends on another \fIsidecar\fP package:
.IP "  \(bu" 4
it shall export dependency side car among own sidecar\&.
.IP "  \(bu" 4
package imports own sidecar via \fRmedium\fP, thus able to export multiple sidecars via one export\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
final consumer uses \fRsidecar\fP or \fRuseSidecar\fP to combine pieces together\&. 
.br

.PP
.SS "Rules"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRUI\fP components might use/import any other \fRUI\fP components
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsidecar\fP could use/import any other \fRsidecar\fP
.PP

.PP
That would form two different code branches, you may load separately - UI first, and effect sidecar later\&. That also leads to a obvious consequence - \fBone sidecar may export all sidecars\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
to decouple \fRsidecars\fP from module exports, and be able to pick "the right" one at any point you have to use \fRexportSidecar(medium, component)\fP to export it, and use the same \fRmedium\fP to import it back\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
this limitation is for \fBlibraries only\fP, as long as in the usercode you might dynamically import whatever and whenever you want\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseMedium\fP is always async - action would be executed in a next tick, or on the logic load\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsidecar\fP is always async - is does not matter have you loaded logic or not - component would be rendered at least in the next tick\&.
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
except \fRmedium\&.read\fP, which synchronously read the data from a medium, and \fRmedium\&.assingSyncMedium\fP which changes \fRuseMedium\fP to be sync\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "SSR and usage tracking"
Sidecar pattern is clear:
.IP "\(bu" 2
you dont need to use/render any \fRsidecars\fP on server\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
you dont have to load \fRsidecars\fP prior main render\&.
.PP

.PP
Thus - no usage tracking, and literally no SSR\&. It's just skipped\&.
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "createMedium()"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: Util\&. Creates shared effect medium for algebraic effect\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Goal: To decouple modules from each other\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Usage: \fRuse\fP in UI side, and \fRassign\fP from side-car\&. All effects would be executed\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Analog: WeakMap, React\&.__SECRET_DOM_DO_NOT_USE_OR_YOU_WILL_BE_FIRED 
.PP
.nf
const medium = createMedium(defaultValue);
const cancelCb = medium\&.useMedium(someData);

// like
useEffect(() => medium\&.useMedium(someData), []);

medium\&.assignMedium(someDataProcessor)

// createSidecarMedium is a helper for createMedium to create a "sidecar" symbol
const effectCar = createSidecarMedium();

.fi
.PP

.PP

.PP
.RS 4
! For consistence \fRuseMedium\fP is async - sidecar load status should not affect function behavior, thus effect would be always executed at least in the "next tick"\&. You may alter this behavior by using \fRmedium\&.assingSyncMedium\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
.SS "exportSidecar(medium, component)"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: HOC
.IP "\(bu" 2
Goal: store \fRcomponent\fP inside \fRmedium\fP and return external wrapper
.IP "\(bu" 2
Solving: decoupling module exports to support exporting multiple sidecars via a single entry point\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Usage: use to export a \fRsidecar\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Analog: WeakMap 
.PP
.nf
import {effectCar} from '\&./medium';
import {EffectComponent} from '\&./Effect';
// !!! \- to prevent Effect from being imported
// `effectCar` medium __have__ to be defined in another file
// const effectCar = createSidecarMedium();
export default exportSidecar(effectCar, EffectComponent);

.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "sidecar(importer)"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: HOC
.IP "\(bu" 2
Goal: React\&.lazy analog for code splitting, but does not require \fRSuspense\fP, might provide error failback\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Usage: like React\&.lazy to load a side-car component\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Analog: React\&.Lazy 
.PP
.nf
import {sidecar} from "use\-sidecar";
const Sidecar =  sidecar(() => import('\&./sidecar'), <span>on fail</span>);

<>
 <Sidecar />
 <UI />
</> 

.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.SS "Importing \fRexportedSidecar\fP"
Would require additional prop to be set - \fR<Sidecar sideCar={effectCar} />\fP
.SS "useSidecar(importer)"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: hook, loads a \fRsideCar\fP using provided \fRimporter\fP which shall follow React\&.lazy API
.IP "\(bu" 2
Goal: to load a side car without displaying any "spinners"\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Usage: load side car for a component
.IP "\(bu" 2
Analog: none 
.PP
.nf
import {useSidecar} from 'use\-sidecar';

const [Car, error] = useSidecar(() => import('\&./sideCar'));
return (
  <>
    {Car ? <Car {\&.\&.\&.props} /> : null}
    <UIComponent {\&.\&.\&.props}>
  </>
); 

.fi
.PP
 
.PP
.SS "Importing \fRexportedSideCar\fP"
You have to specify \fBeffect medium\fP to read data from, as long as \fBexport itself is empty\fP\&. 
.PP
.nf
import {useSidecar} from 'use\-sidecar';

/* medium\&.js: */ export const effectCar = useMedium({});
/* sideCar\&.js: */export default exportSidecar(effectCar, EffectComponent);

const [Car, error] = useSidecar(() => import('\&./sideCar'), effectCar); 
return (
  <>
    {Car ? <Car {\&.\&.\&.props} /> : null}
    <UIComponent {\&.\&.\&.props}>
  </>
);

.fi
.PP
.SS "renderCar(Component)"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Type: HOC, moves renderProp component to a side channel
.IP "\(bu" 2
Goal: Provide render prop support, ie defer component loading keeping tree untouched\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Usage: Provide \fRdefaults\fP and use them until sidecar is loaded letting you code split (non visual) render-prop component
.IP "\(bu" 2
Analog: - Analog: code split library like \fRreact-imported-library\fP or \fR@loadable/lib\fP\&. 
.PP
.nf
import {renderCar, sidecar} from "use\-sidecar";
const RenderCar = renderCar(
  // will move side car to a side channel
  sidecar(() => import('react\-powerplug')\&.then(imports => imports\&.Value)),
  // default render props
  [{value: 0}]  
);

<RenderCar>
  {({value}) => <span>{value}</span>}
</RenderCar>

.fi
.PP

.PP
.SS "setConfig(config)"
.PP
.nf
setConfig({
  onError, // sets default error handler
});
.fi
.PP
.SH "Examples"
.PP
.SS "Deferred effect"
Let's imagine - on element focus you have to do "something", for example focus anther element
.SS "Original code"
.PP
.nf
onFocus = event => {
  if (event\&.currentTarget === event\&.target) {
    document\&.querySelectorAll('button', event\&.currentTarget)
  }
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Sidecar code"
.IP "3." 4
Use medium (yes, \&.3) 
.PP
.nf
// we are calling medium with an original event as an argument
const onFocus = event => focusMedium\&.useMedium(event);

.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "2." 4
Define reaction 
.PP
.nf
// in a sidecar

// we are setting handler for the effect medium
// effect is complicated \- we are skipping event "bubbling", 
// and focusing some button inside a parent
focusMedium\&.assignMedium(event => {
  if (event\&.currentTarget === event\&.target) {
    document\&.querySelectorAll('button', event\&.currentTarget)
  }
});

.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "1." 4
Create medium Having these constrains - we have to clone \fRevent\fP, as long as React would eventually reuse SyntheticEvent, thus not preserve \fRtarget\fP and \fRcurrentTarget\fP\&. 
.PP
.nf
// 
const focusMedium = createMedium(null, event => ({\&.\&.\&.event}));

.fi
.PP
 Now medium side effect is ok to be async
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP: \fREffect for react-focus-lock\fP - 1kb UI, 4kb sidecar
.SS "Medium callback"
Like a library level code splitting
.SS "Original code"
.PP
.nf
import {x, y} from '\&./utils';

useEffect(() => {
  if (x()) {
    y()
  }
}, []);
.fi
.PP
.SS "Sidecar code"
.PP
.nf
// medium
const utilMedium = createMedium();

// utils
const x = () => { /* \&.\&.\&. */};
const y = () => { /* \&.\&.\&. */};

// medium will callback with exports exposed
utilMedium\&.assignMedium(cb => cb({
 x, y
}));


// UI
// not importing x and y from the module system, but would be given via callback
useEffect(() => {
  utilMedium\&.useMedium(({x,y}) => {
      if (x()) {
        y()
      }
  })
}, []);
.fi
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Hint: there is a easy way to type it 
.PP
.nf
const utilMedium = createMedium<(cb: typeof import('\&./utils')) => void>();

.fi
.PP

.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP: \fRCallback API for react-focus-lock\fP
.SS "Split effects"
Lets take an example from a Google - Calendar app, with view and logic separated\&. To be honest - it's not easy to extract logic from application like calendar - usually it's tight coupled\&.
.SS "Original code"
.PP
.nf
const CalendarUI = () => { 
  const [date, setDate] = useState();
  const onButtonClick = useCallback(() => setDate(Date\&.now), []);
  
  return (
    <>
     <input type="date" onChange={setDate} value={date} />
     <input type="button" onClick={onButtonClick}>Set Today</button>
    </>
  )
}
.fi
.PP
 
.SS "Sidecar code"
.PP
.nf
const CalendarUI = () => {
  const [events, setEvents] = useState({});
  const [date, setDate] = useState();
  
  return (
    <>
     <Sidecar setDate={setDate} setEvents={setEvents}/>
     <UILayout {\&.\&.\&.events} date={date}/>
    </>
  )
}

const UILayout = ({onDateChange, onButtonClick, date}) => (
  <>
      <input type="date" onChange={onDateChange} value={date} />
      <input type="button" onClick={onButtonClick}>Set Today</button>
  </>
);

// in a sidecar
// we are providing callbacks back to UI
const Sidecar = ({setDate, setEvents}) => {
  useEffect(() => setEvents({
      onDateChange:setDate,
      onButtonClick: () => setDate(Date\&.now),
  }), []);
  
  return null;
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
While in this example this looks a bit, you know, strange - there are 3 times more code that in the original example - that would make a sense for a real Calendar, especially if some helper library, like \fRmoment\fP, has been used\&.

